# =============================================================================
# Peak Kinetics — backend environment template
# =============================================================================
# Every env var the Spring Boot backend reads, grouped by deployment layer.
#
# Usage:
#   - Production (Fly.io): translate each line below into a `flyctl secrets set`
#     call. Do NOT upload this file to Fly directly.
#   - Local dev: copy this file to `backend/.env` and fill in real values.
#     `.env` is gitignored. Or `export VAR=value` in your shell — the local
#     profile (`application-local.yml`) provides safe defaults for most vars,
#     so you only need to set what you want to actually exercise.
#
# Order of layers reflects what's required vs optional:
#   Layer 1 = required to boot
#   Layer 2 = security (recommended on Day 1; missing = fail-open)
#   Layer 3 = feature flags (off by default; only set when turning on)
#   Layer 4 = overrides (defaults are fine; set only if you need to change them)
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# LAYER 1 — REQUIRED for the app to start
# =============================================================================
# Spring will fail to start if any of these are missing or unresolvable.

# --- Database (Aiven Postgres) -----------------------------------------------
# Get from Aiven console → your service → Connection Information → JDBC URI.
# Format: jdbc:postgresql://<host>:<port>/<database>?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://<host>.aivencloud.com:<port>/defaultdb?sslmode=require
DATABASE_USERNAME=avnadmin
DATABASE_PASSWORD=<aiven-service-password>

# --- JWT signing key ---------------------------------------------------------
# 32+ char random string. Generate fresh per environment:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating this invalidates every active session immediately.
JWT_SECRET=<generate-with-openssl-rand-base64-48>

# --- Resend (transactional email) --------------------------------------------
# Get from Resend dashboard → API Keys → Create API Key (Full access).
# Sender domain must be verified in Resend (DKIM/SPF/return-path published in
# Cloudflare DNS for whichever domain you're sending from).
RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Optional but recommended — overrides safe defaults baked into the app.
RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS='PEAK KINETICS PT <noReply@peakkineticspt.net>'
RESEND_REPLY_TO=info@peakkineticspt.com


# =============================================================================
# LAYER 2 — SECURITY (set on Day 1 unless you accept fail-open behaviour)
# =============================================================================
# Each of these has an empty default. When blank, the corresponding security
# filter silently disables itself — the app still starts and runs, just with
# the protection turned off. See docs/cloudflare-domains.md Step 5a for
# click-by-click instructions on where to find each value in the Cloudflare UI.

# --- Cloudflare Turnstile (anti-bot on contact + review forms) --------------
# Cloudflare account → Turnstile → Add widget → copy the SECRET KEY (not the
# Site Key — Site Key is for the frontend bundle).
# Without this: TurnstileService.verify() returns true for everyone — bots
# can spam /api/messages and review submissions.
CLOUDFLARE_TURNSTILE_SECRET=0x4AAAAAAA_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Cloudflare Access (gates /admin/* routes) -------------------------------
# Zero Trust dashboard → Settings (team domain shown in the form
# <team>.cloudflareaccess.com — copy the full hostname, no scheme/path).
# Audience is generated when you create an Access Application; copy the
# "Application Audience (AUD) Tag" from the app's Overview tab.
# Without these: CloudflareAccessFilter is disabled and admin routes are
# protected only by JWT.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=<your-team>.cloudflareaccess.com
CLOUDFLARE_ACCESS_AUDIENCE=<aud-tag-from-cloudflare-access-app>


# =============================================================================
# LAYER 3 — FEATURE FLAGS (off by default; flip on when each is fully wired)
# =============================================================================

# --- Twilio (SMS sending) ----------------------------------------------------
# Disabled by default. The NoOpSmsService bean is active and the Twilio classes
# are never loaded. Flip the flag + set the three creds when ready to send.
TWILIO_ENABLED=false
# When TWILIO_ENABLED=true, all three of these are required:
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=+1XXXXXXXXXX

# --- Google Business Profile (review sync) -----------------------------------
# Disabled by default. The NoOpGoogleBusinessProfileService returns an empty
# review list and the google-api-client classes are never loaded. The OAuth
# refresh-token + Business Profile API setup is non-trivial — leave off until
# fully validated in a non-prod env.
GOOGLE_BUSINESS_ENABLED=false
# When GOOGLE_BUSINESS_ENABLED=true, all five of these are required:
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=1//xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
GOOGLE_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT=accounts/1234567890
GOOGLE_BUSINESS_LOCATION=locations/9876543210
# (Note: GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY / GOOGLE_PLACES_PLACE_ID are intentionally
#  absent — the Places API can only return ≤5 reviews per place; the Business
#  Profile flow above is the only way to get the full feed.)

# --- Cloudflare R2 (object storage for uploads) ------------------------------
# Not needed in Week 1 — uploads go to the Fly volume mounted at /app/uploads.
# In Week 2, set all five of these and FileUploadController switches to R2.
# If any of the five is blank, the app falls back to local storage.
R2_ENDPOINT=https://<account-hash>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
R2_BUCKET=peak-kinetics-uploads
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://cdn.peakkineticspt.com

# --- Grafana Cloud OTLP (logs / traces / metrics export) ---------------------
# Disabled by default. When enabled, the OTLP exporters in application-prod.yml
# ship to Grafana Cloud. Auth header format: 'Basic <base64(instanceID:token)>'
# — generate it from the "OpenTelemetry" connection page in Grafana Cloud.
GRAFANA_OTLP_ENABLED=false
GRAFANA_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://otlp-gateway-prod-us-east-<N>.grafana.net/otlp
GRAFANA_OTLP_AUTH='Basic <base64-encoded-instance-id-and-token>'
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf
OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES='service.name=peak-kinetics,service.namespace=peakkineticspt,deployment.environment=prod'


# =============================================================================
# LAYER 4 — OVERRIDES (defaults are correct for prod; only set if changing)
# =============================================================================

# --- Local upload storage path -----------------------------------------------
# Default in prod is /app/uploads (matches the `peak_uploads` Fly volume mount
# in fly.toml). Only change if you've remounted the volume elsewhere.
# STORAGE_UPLOAD_DIR=/app/uploads

# --- CORS allowed origins ----------------------------------------------------
# Defaults already include peakkineticspt.com, www., *.peakkineticspt.com,
# and *.pages.dev. Override only if the frontend hostname changes.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://peakkineticspt.com,https://www.peakkineticspt.com
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGIN_PATTERNS=https://*.peakkineticspt.com,https://*.pages.dev

# --- Spring profile ----------------------------------------------------------
# Set in fly.toml [env] block already (SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod).
# For local dev, use `local`. For tests, use `test` (set automatically by
# the Maven Surefire plugin).
# SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod


# =============================================================================
# Convert this file into the actual flyctl deploy commands
# =============================================================================
# Layer 1 (required) — run this once, all values come from password manager:
#
# flyctl secrets set --app peak-kinetics-api \
#   DATABASE_URL='jdbc:postgresql://...' \
#   DATABASE_USERNAME='...' \
#   DATABASE_PASSWORD='...' \
#   JWT_SECRET="$(openssl rand -base64 48)" \
#   RESEND_API_KEY='re_...' \
#   RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS='PEAK KINETICS PT <noReply@peakkineticspt.net>' \
#   RESEND_REPLY_TO='info@peakkineticspt.com'
#
# Layer 2 (security) — recommended on Day 1:
#
# flyctl secrets set --app peak-kinetics-api \
#   CLOUDFLARE_TURNSTILE_SECRET='0x4AAA...' \
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN='<your-team>.cloudflareaccess.com' \
#   CLOUDFLARE_ACCESS_AUDIENCE='<aud-tag>'
#
# Layer 3 — only when turning each feature on (each is independent).
# After all required + security secrets are set:
#
# flyctl secrets list --app peak-kinetics-api    # should show 10 entries
# flyctl deploy --app peak-kinetics-api --remote-only
#
# See docs/cloudflare-domains.md for the full walkthrough.
